# DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself)

When you find the **same logic copy-pasted** in two or more places, extract it into a private helper. This is especially important when the duplication touches index math, data manipulation, or anything that could silently drift if one copy is updated and the other isn't.

When introducing a new function, scan nearby code for similar patterns first; prefer extending an existing helper over creating a parallel one.

```python
# BAD: same val-from-train carving logic copy-pasted in two splitters
def country_holdout_split(...):
    ...
    if not val_idx and val_size > 0:
        meta_train = meta.iloc[train_idx].reset_index(drop=True)
        tr_sub, vl_sub = _group_split(meta_train, val_size, seed, ...)
        val_idx = [train_idx[i] for i in vl_sub]
        train_idx = [train_idx[i] for i in tr_sub]

def temporal_split(...):
    ...
    if not val_idx and val_size > 0:
        meta_train = meta.iloc[train_idx].reset_index(drop=True)
        tr_sub, vl_sub = _group_split(meta_train, val_size, seed, ...)
        val_idx = [train_idx[i] for i in vl_sub]
        train_idx = [train_idx[i] for i in tr_sub]

# GOOD: shared logic lives in one place
def _carve_val_from_train(meta, train_idx, val_size, seed, group_col, label_col):
    ...

def country_holdout_split(...):
    ...
    if not val_idx:
        train_idx, val_idx = _carve_val_from_train(meta, train_idx, val_size, seed, ...)

def temporal_split(...):
    ...
    if not val_idx:
        train_idx, val_idx = _carve_val_from_train(meta, train_idx, val_size, seed, ...)
```

## When NOT to DRY

Don't force shared abstractions when the similarity is **superficial**. Two snippets that happen to look alike but represent different concepts (e.g. "load model config" vs "load experiment config") should stay separate — premature consolidation creates worse coupling than duplication.

Rule of thumb: extract once you see the **third** occurrence, or sooner if the duplicated code is non-trivial (>3 lines of meaningful logic).
